Routing: is the process that routers use to determine the path that packets should take to reach their destination. Routers store routes to all their known destination in a 
routing table. There are dynamic routing (using protocols like OSPF) and static routing (manual setup). 

Match: a route matches a packet's destination if the paclet's destination IP address is part of the network specified in the route. If there are different matching routes 
for a single ip, the most specific one will be used. For example, 192.168.1.1 goes to a router that has a connected route of 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.1.1/32. They're both
matching route, but the later is more specific, so the router will use that one.

When an IP address is configured on an interface and enable, two routes are automatically added to the routing table: connected route (network address of the subnet, will 
learn this later) and local route (exact individual IP /32).

Note the difference between routers and switches:

Routers: uses IP, works with packets, looks for best matching route otherwise will drop packet
Switches: uses MAC, works with frames, looks for exact MAC, otherwise will flood the network.
